1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,210
Good food goes to waste while community kitchens need meals.

2
00:00:03,210 --> 00:00:08,773
The hard part is coordinating changing constraints without losing commitments people have already made.

3
00:00:08,773 --> 00:00:13,588
This is Second Serve, a shared food rescue workspace for people and their browser agents.

4
00:00:14,588 --> 00:00:17,206
The website exposes six real Web MCP tools.

5
00:00:17,206 --> 00:00:21,834
The browser discovered these tools and called them during this walkthrough.

6
00:00:21,834 --> 00:00:27,619
An agent can read the workspace, compare plans, explain rejected matches, and stage proposals.

7
00:00:27,619 --> 00:00:31,759
These calls use the same application state as the visible controls.

8
00:00:32,759 --> 00:00:38,352
Here, a real stage plan tool call puts a 480 portion proposal directly on the board.

9
00:00:38,352 --> 00:00:45,876
A min cost maximum flow solver checks capacity, diets, declared allergen exclusions, and estimated time windows.

10
00:00:45,876 --> 00:00:47,407
The agent can propose.

11
00:00:47,407 --> 00:00:50,270
Approval stays in the visible review flow.

12
00:00:51,270 --> 00:00:56,849
After approving, I protect the 120 portion assignment from The Green Kitchen to Hope Community.

13
00:00:56,849 --> 00:00:59,844
This represents a commitment already made locally.

14
00:00:59,844 --> 00:01:05,129
The planner must preserve that exact quantity and destination when the situation changes.

15
00:01:05,129 --> 00:01:09,181
If a protected commitment becomes impossible, it reports a conflict.

16
00:01:10,181 --> 00:01:13,751
Now Open Table can only receive 30 portions instead of 100.

17
00:01:13,751 --> 00:01:18,893
A real Web MCP capacity update changes the board and flags the old plan immediately.

18
00:01:18,893 --> 00:01:24,398
The current revision protects against stale agent calls overwriting newer human decisions.

19
00:01:25,398 --> 00:01:28,964
The agent stages a fresh feasible plan for 410 portions.

20
00:01:28,964 --> 00:01:32,975
The protected 120 stay exactly where the coordinator put them.

21
00:01:32,975 --> 00:01:36,732
Seventy portions remain unassigned, with a visible reason.

22
00:01:36,732 --> 00:01:42,080
Second Serve exposes the shortfall rather than pretending every meal can be placed.

23
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:47,009
The activity trail distinguishes human decisions from agent calls.

24
00:01:47,009 --> 00:01:52,129
Read only what if tools let an agent explore alternatives without changing the board.

25
00:01:52,129 --> 00:01:54,808
Invalid inputs fail without partial updates.

26
00:01:54,808 --> 00:02:00,284
The tests cover constraints, protected commitments, revision conflicts, and human approval.

27
00:02:01,284 --> 00:02:07,084
After another human review, the approved plan is available as a structured manifest or CSV handoff.

28
00:02:07,084 --> 00:02:11,888
The app runs without an account or API key, and supports importing your own data.

29
00:02:11,888 --> 00:02:15,285
These demo records and travel estimates are illustrative.

30
00:02:15,285 --> 00:02:17,043
No deliveries are dispatched.

31
00:02:17,043 --> 00:02:21,378
Second Serve shows agents working with our decisions, where we make them.
